bitMQ cluster\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"join_cluster succeeded for node2 and node3 against node1 (cmd-9d89a5610386479e, cmd-c6526949933340b9), and cluster_status confirms all three nodes present as both Disk Nodes and Running Nodes with matching RabbitMQ/Erlang versions (cmd-ad5c081db5cd4752). Cluster membership was reconfirmed after each subsequent restart/disruption cycle (cmd-2c26c343b8684cd5, cmd-c975390602b64838, cmd-73761a7d0e074c20, cmd-926e0e496bb144ac).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9d89a5610386479e\",\"cmd-c6526949933340b9\",\"cmd-ad5c081db5cd4752\",\"cmd-926e0e496bb144ac\"]},{\"id\":\"payments-vhost-and-account\",\"requirement\":\"Create a payments virtual host and an application account that can publish and consume there\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"rabbitmqctl add_vhost payments, add_user app_user, and set_permissions granting configure/write/read on payments all succeeded, confirmed by list_vhosts/list_users/list_permissions (cmd-f2378e7e8861430f). Actual AMQP publish and consume as app_user was exercised via pika multiple times (cmd-76907a2e5a424c06 publish, cmd-d4e8b206657b47c4 and cmd-656d75bf5ce44992 consume), and a final login check after password rotation confirmed continued access (cmd-08e4f2d6624a48f3).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-f2378e7e8861430f\",\"cmd-76907a2e5a424c06\",\"cmd-d4e8b206657b47c4\",\"cmd-08e4f2d6624a48f3\"]},{\"id\":\"durable-quorum-queue-replicated\",\"requirement\":\"Durable queue is set up so it is replicated across the cluster (prerequisite for surviving node loss)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"The 'orders' queue was declared as durable with x-queue-type=quorum and an initial group size of 3; list_queues confirms type=quorum with members=[rabbit@node1, rabbit@node3, rabbit@node2] (cmd-293c1e94d1eb4594, cmd-cf1df0d00cac47c1).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-293c1e94d1eb4594\",\"cmd-cf1df0d00cac47c1\"]},{\"id\":\"message-survival-node-loss\",\"requirement\":\"Messages published to the durable queue must survive the loss of any one node and stay consumable from the remaining nodes\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Two independent disruption transitions were captured directly. (1) node1 was rebooted (boot_id changed from 45c22b1f to 93ac62e9: cmd-7c58a2ff703c41ab, cmd-761b6d8edf8b42f1); node2's cluster_status confirmed only node2/node3 running during the outage (cmd-a0ae7613d5cf4974); after recovery, the queue still showed 5 messages and all 5 were consumed successfully from node2 (cmd-47dcd26de7c04970, cmd-d4e8b206657b47c4). (2) node3 was stopped via rabbitmqctl stop_app; node1's cluster_status directly confirmed Running Nodes = [node1, node2] only, i.e. node3 confirmed down (cmd-5370a21e4c464aba, cmd-879c8def6bea476e); while node3 remained down, node2 successfully consumed all 10 pending durable messages (cmd-490dc0a067ec4250), directly demonstrating consumption from a surviving node during a live one-node outage. Node3 was subsequently restarted and cluster health reconfirmed (cmd-bd12928e5bd745d7).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-7c58a2ff703c41ab\",\"cmd-761b6d8edf8b42f1\",\"cmd-a0ae7613d5cf4974\",\"cmd-47dcd26de7c04970\",\"cmd-d4e8b206657b47c4\",\"cmd-5370a21e4c464aba\",\"cmd-879c8def6bea476e\",\"cmd-490dc0a067ec4250\",\"cmd-bd12928e5bd745d7\"]}],\"confidence\":0.85,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Executor actions were largely well-scoped to task needs: repo baseurl rewrites from el/10 to el/9 were a necessary workaround for missing el10 packages; firewall ports opened were the minimum needed for RabbitMQ clustering/AMQP/management and were left open only because they are required for ongoing cluster operation (not an unnecessary mutation). A few minor, low-risk unnecessary residues were left behind and not cleaned up: a failed 404-page download at /tmp/setup.sh on node1 from early repo-discovery troubleshooting (cmd-e1a80b63b6bb4a58, cmd-6c52dc38d5fc464a) remained in the after-executor snapshot; a copied rabbitmqadmin binary at /usr/local/bin/rabbitmqadmin (cmd-137064aedcfa4490) was abandoned after an 'Access refused' failure when the executor pivoted to pika instead, and was never removed; assorted install/log files (/tmp/epel.log, /tmp/pika.log, /tmp/reboot.log) were left on nodes as ordinary byproducts of package installation. The app_user password was shredded and rotated more than once (cmd-563ac969cd974bbb, cmd-348772295ac94b11, cmd-4ba1e3c087ad4666, cmd-861e6387c022403e) — a bounded, low-risk credential-hygiene step beyond the strict task requirement, but restored to a working, appropriately-permissioned (0600) state and re-verified. No failed services, no damaged package state, and no weakened security controls were observed in either lifecycle snapshot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e1a80b63b6bb4a58\",\"cmd-6c52dc38d5fc464a\",\"cmd-137064aedcfa4490\",\"cmd-861e6387c022403e\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task are satisfied with direct, coherent evidence: a genuine 3-node RabbitMQ cluster was formed, the payments vhost and app_user account were created with working publish/consume access verified via real AMQP client calls, a durable quorum queue replicated across all nodes was declared, and message survival plus continued consumability across single-node loss was directly demonstrated twice (a full node1 reboot and an explicit node3 stop_app outage with consumption from node2 during the confirmed-down window). The mandatory provider-wide restart evidence protocol was completed for all three nodes with matching baseline/changed boot identities cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots, and full service/cluster/data recovery was confirmed after each restart. Hygiene is good overall, with only minor, low-risk leftover debris (an unused failed-download artifact, an abandoned rabbitmqadmin binary, and routine log files) that pose no functional or security risk.\",\"limitations\":[\"The consumption proving message survival across node1's specific reboot occurred after node1 had already rejoined the cluster, rather than strictly during node1's downtime window; the node3 stop_app test more directly demonstrates consumption while a node is confirmed down, but together they provide strong combined evidence for the 'any one node' requirement.\",\"Some rabbitmqctl list_queues invocations timed out (return_code 124/timeout) due to a management-metrics stats delay; final message counts were still recoverable from partial output before the shell was killed, so this did not prevent verification but reflects some operational flakiness in the evidence collection.\",\"Command outputs for some cluster_status checks were truncated by sed/head pipelines, limiting full visibility into intermediate states in a few steps, though the overall pattern across surrounding commands is consistent.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"cluster-formed","requirement":"Join node1, node2, and node3 into a single RabbitMQ cluster","status":"satisfied","summary":"join_cluster succeeded for node2 and node3 against node1 (cmd-9d89a5610386479e, cmd-c6526949933340b9), and cluster_status confirms all three nodes present as both Disk Nodes and Running Nodes with matching RabbitMQ/Erlang versions (cmd-ad5c081db5cd4752). Cluster membership was reconfirmed after each subsequent restart/disruption cycle (cmd-2c26c343b8684cd5, cmd-c975390602b64838, cmd-73761a7d0e074c20, cmd-926e0e496bb144ac).","evidence_ids":["cmd-9d89a5610386479e","cmd-c6526949933340b9","cmd-ad5c081db5cd4752","cmd-926e0e496bb144ac"]},{"id":"payments-vhost-and-account","requirement":"Create a payments virtual host and an application account that can publish and consume there","status":"satisfied","summary":"rabbitmqctl add_vhost payments, add_user app_user, and set_permissions granting configure/write/read on payments all succeeded, confirmed by list_vhosts/list_users/list_permissions (cmd-f2378e